  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nSurface low currently over the Central Plains will ever so slowly \nchurn eastward through this weekend. Simultaneously, blocking high \nover Ontario will slide eastward through tonight, though reinforcing \nblocking high will propagate towards Ontario from central Canada \nthis weekend. While elevated dewpoints (for this time of year) \nand abundant low-level stratus will be common, including some \ndrizzle in the far south central this afternoon, back to back \nhighs will allow dry air to win out across most of the state \nthrough this weekend. There are a few exceptions, however. \n\nFirst, shortwave energy today into tonight may allow additional \nshower and possibly thunderstorm development mainly in northwestern \nNorth Dakota, but also potentially the far north central along the \nInternational Border, and the far west central along the ND/MT \nborder. Instability is generally expected to remain less than a 1000 \nJ/kg with up to 30 kts of deep layer shear, so severe weather isn't \nexpected. That said, small hail remains possible. Second, along\nthe gradient between the aforementioned highs and low, which \nincludes mainly areas south and west of the Missouri River, more\norganized rain/showers are possible Saturday through Sunday. \nThese showers could expand into the northwest and further east \nas well, though confidence decreases rapidly the further east \none goes. Once precipitation ends Sunday night, mostly dry \nconditions are expected through Tuesday before low chances \nreturn Wednesday afternoon through Wednesday night. \n\nSeasonable high temperatures, albeit below average, are \nexpected through this weekend with highs mostly in the 60s.\nLows, on the other hand, will mostly remain on the higher side\nof average. For next week, a slight warming trend is favored\nwith highs returning into the 70s for most locations, most days,\nby Wednesday.",
